/-!
# Instances of the reflected tile checker (J2)

The certificate literals and their `decide +kernel` theorems below were produced by the
untrusted emitter `tools/emit_tile_certificate.ts` (its arguments are recorded in each
docstring). The emitter only proposes integers; the kernel evaluates `checkTile` on them,
and `checkTile_sound` turns acceptance into the stated dynamics.

* `minusOnePeriodTwoTile` re-certifies the existing period-two critical tile about `-1`
  at grid `2^-64`. `minusOnePeriodTwoTile_complex_trap` recovers, for every complex
  parameter in `periodTwoRationalTile`, the same seed disk `‖z‖ ≤ 1/16`, entry index `2`,
  and contraction bound `1/2` as `periodTwoRationalTile_complex_trap`, and in addition
  states explicitly the return fixed-point equation and that every fixed point of the
  two-step return in the seed disk equals the certified point.
* `rabbitPeriodThreeTile` is a period-three smoke test. Its parameter-disk center is the
  dyadic `(-2260854478748770784 + 13740274379125515960 i) / 2^64`, the nearest grid point to
  the decimal `-0.12256116687665 + 0.74486176661974i` (near the rabbit center, a root of
  `c³ + 2c² + c + 1`); parameter radius `2^-20`, seed radius `1/16`, contraction bound `1/2`.
  The stored critical orbit returns next to the cycle point near `0` every three steps, so
  any entry index divisible by three works; `30` was chosen to exercise a 31-entry critical
  table (ten returns). That the true cubic root lies in this disk is *not* proved here.
* `boundaryPeriodThreeTile` is a period-three tile in the rabbit component near its boundary
  (multiplier modulus about `0.9`), parameter radius `2^-20`, seed radius about `10^-3`,
  critical entry at iterate `150`. With the optional lower chain it certifies
  `0.89 ≤ ‖λ(ζ)‖ ≤ 0.91` throughout the parameter disk.
* Four refusals: an over-ambitious contraction bound, a corrupted radius witness, a
  corrupted critical error witness, and an over-claimed lower multiplier bound all make the
  respective checker return `false`.

All uniqueness is within the seed disk only. Nothing here concerns binary64 arithmetic.
-/

